Key results and impact

2026 was the year Discover's payments stack began moving into Capital One, and I spent it at the seam between the two companies, on the two products where the move was hardest: Bill Pay and Wires. In both I was the engineer who knew how the Discover side actually worked, and I used that to turn migration plans into things that ran in production. The clearest proof is the August associate pilot. As Bill Pay SME I led the Discover-side architecture and execution end to end, from the conversion flows and migration-day timelines through segmentation, data ownership, dry runs, the enrollment restrictions and shutoffs, the deconversion-file transfers, go/no-go, fallout analysis and Capital One validation. I designed separate controls for Bill Pay access and eBill enrollment so the two could migrate on different timings without touching customers who were not moving, and on the wires side I delivered the mobile international-wire restrictions, disclosure updates and the wire maintenance page the pilot needed. The pilot succeeded, and more importantly it left behind a plan that is repeatable for the larger customer waves.

Alongside the pilot I removed a whole class of risk that nobody had budgeted for. With AI-assisted development I built an analytics dashboard that turns about 400,000 FIS deconversion records into answers in roughly thirty seconds, replacing reports we had been waiting on and giving the team customer, payee, funding-account and payment views, duplicate detection and drill-down on demand. That tool is what let me find 51,000 funding accounts closed on the Discover side but still live in FIS, match them against the pilot population, and narrow a $20,000 vendor cleanup quote to 473 records, which I then closed with a batch I built in a few hours and ran overnight in production. The same tooling reconciled the 200,000-record mega dry run, produced the remapping data for the MMA-as-checking errors and defined the MMA shutoff rules. The analysis that used to take days now takes seconds, and reconciliation going into the November customer-pilot wave is stronger for it.

The work I am most invested in is the DFS OPF Convergence program. Capital One must move Discover's full network and corporate wire volume, settled today through FIS OPF, off the DFS mainframe by the May 2027 shutdown and onto Payment Exchange, keeping OPF only as a bridge until Capital One connects to the banks directly. I joined as the engineer who knows Discover's wire channels, systems and ways of working, and I am the Discover voice in the pod connecting Payment Exchange to OPF. I translate how things work at Discover into designs Capital One can build, test assumptions against the FIS platform rather than on paper, and push for the simplest design that survives contact with the live business, including helping retire a queued submission path that added risk without value. I own the pain.001 generation and OPF submission design, keep its architecture records aligned and carry the open questions to FIS, and I treat the OPF bridge as a step toward direct bank connectivity so that what we build now carries into the next phase rather than being thrown away. Earlier in the year I represented Outbound Wires in the migration discussions, partnered with Inbound Wires on routing design and evaluated the proposed Excel-to-ISO 20022 conversion, which is the groundwork that put me in this seat.

None of this was allowed to come at the expense of the platforms we still run. I designed a standalone WireMock sidecar pattern that removed test-runtime dependencies from production bundles and had it adopted across all seven team-owned APIs rather than fixing each one, diagnosed a certificate-trust issue in a day and fixed it across four Bill Pay APIs in three, and coordinated the VulnHunter high-severity remediation across six applications during the repository migration, with a reusable re-scan skill, AI-assisted summaries and estimates, a shared tracker, and daily huddles where I substantiated the DFS-versus-Capital One architectural false positives so they were removed rather than worked. Those findings closed a week ahead of the deadline. I also supported the JDK 21 and Spring Boot 4 migration and the certificate renewals. And in the joint Discover and Capital One hackathon, our team took NeuroSecure, an AI-enabled scam-prevention concept, from idea to a working, judge-ready application in two days and placed second among 150 teams; I evaluated our ideas against the field, wrote the decision walkthrough, designed the frontend, contributed the multi-shield architecture and helped develop the AI system prompts.

Beyond the five results, a few things I would call out. I have built an AI-assisted way of working that turns meeting recordings, design documents and vendor contracts into decisions, actions and design records the pod can rely on, so that nothing agreed in a workshop or a vendor call is lost and the architecture pages stay consistent with what was actually decided. I wrote the epic and the stories for the Wire II work from the program's own capability definitions so the pod could estimate and start building without waiting. I have been the one raising the vendor-facing questions that would otherwise surface late, from payload encryption options on the FIS gateway to message-version compatibility and idempotency handling, and documenting the answers where the team can find them. And I have done all of this while learning Capital One's platform, processes and ISO 20022 from a standing start, which I take as a sign of how quickly I can be useful in new territory.

Competency strengths and opportunities

The strength underneath all of this is ownership across company boundaries. I take responsibility for the Discover side end to end, including the parts nobody has assigned, and I have become the person Capital One and FIS come to when they need to know how Discover really works. I simplify through clarity: the diagrams, infographics and walkthroughs I produce let business teams, vendors and engineers validate the same design together, and decisions move faster because people can see them. I combine depth with speed and reach for the reusable pattern before the one-off fix, which is why one sidecar pattern covered seven APIs and one dashboard replaced a stack of reports. I verify against real data and real systems and I say plainly what is not yet proven. I use AI as a force multiplier rather than a novelty, and I think a step ahead: the wave after the pilot, the phase after the bridge, the pattern after the fix.

The opportunities are the other side of the same coin. Payment Exchange, Capital One's cloud and security processes and its architecture governance are newer to me than the Discover stack; I am closing that gap fast, and going deeper will let me shape designs rather than mainly validate them from the Discover side. My input lands well inside the teams I work with, and the next step is to carry it earlier and more formally into the architecture forums and vendor conversations so decisions are made once rather than revisited. And several of this year's results depended on me personally; I want to turn more of what I know into documentation, patterns and onboarding so the migration does not bottleneck on one engineer.

Development focus and support needed

My focus for the coming year is to own the Phase 1 wire path through production and the May 2027 shutdown, and to position that work so it carries straight into direct bank connectivity in Phase 2; to build hands-on depth in Payment Exchange, ISO 20022 and Capital One's platform and security processes with the aim of leading architecture decisions for payments integration; to mature the AI-assisted engineering practices I used this year into shared, repeatable tooling for the team; and to grow as a technical leader across the two organizations through clear written design records, early escalation of risk and mentoring the engineers joining the convergence work. The support that would make the biggest difference is a standing seat in the Capital One architecture and vendor forums where the convergence decisions are made, timely resolution of the environment, repository and vendor-access dependencies that gate the Phase 1 build, mentorship from senior Capital One payments architects as I deepen on their platform, and visibility into the Phase 2 roadmap so that what I build now is designed for it.
